  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Sunday)\nIssued at 246 PM CDT MON SEP 7 2026\n\nA very interesting mesoscale setup for late summer is currently\nongoing, with a strong easterly wedge moving in from GA. Surface\nhigh pressure centered over the Appalachians and mid-Atlantic\nregion will keep a strong wedge in place at least through\nmidweek. Isentropic upglide due to easterly/southeasterly winds\nat the surface and northerly to northwesterly winds aloft (700 to\n500mb) has allowed for a stratus cloud deck to develop across\neastern and northeastern Alabama, along with much of northern\nGeorgia. With the wedge and clouds moving in from Georgia,\ntemperatures are actually still in the 80s at Anniston and\nGadsden, while hot and humid low to mid 90s are observed\nelsewhere.  Moisture pooling ahead of the wedge front has\nactually spiked dewpoints into the low and mid 70s along and west\nof I-65, pushing heat indices close to Heat Advisory criteria. \nWe should see the easterly flow gradually work to the west this\nafternoon to provide a few degrees of \"relief\" but conditions\nwill still feel quite oppressive through the afternoon hours\nbefore easterly winds of 10 to 15mph arrive late this afternoon\nand into the evening hours.\n\nIn terms of rain chances this afternoon and evening, we'll still\nhave to keep an eye on a few storms this afternoon that will be\ncapable of producing gusty winds again, but coverage will be\nlimited north of the I-20 corridor due to a slug of dry air\nmoving in aloft and capping the environment.  The dry air was\nvery pronounced on the 12z KBMX sounding above 500mb.  With very\nsteep low-level lapse rates, we'll see mainly pulse storms this\nafternoon with updrafts quickly building and collapsing. With the\ndry air aloft, microburst-type wind gusts of 40 to 50mph are\nlikely within stronger storms.  As storms diminish with the loss\nof daytime heating this evening, easterly winds are expected to\ncontinue overnight, instead of the usual calm or variable winds\nwe've been used to this summer. The strong gradient from the\neasterly wedge is forecast to keep the boundary layer mixed, with\nsustained easterly winds of 5 to 10mph. A few spots in the higher\nelevations across the eastern and northeastern counties may\nactually observe some wind gusts between 15 and 20mph overnight.\n\nThe easterly wedge will remain in place through the day on\nWednesday, as the 500mb ridge builds in aloft. Rain chances are\nforecast around zero as a result on Tuesday, with 20 to 30%\nchances of a shower or storm along and south of I-85 by\nWednesday. Otherwise we'll stay hot in the 90s, but stay below\nHeat Advisory criteria thanks to slightly drier air moving in\nwith the easterly wedge. Guidance today is hanging on to the\n500mb ridge a little longer through the end of the week which may\nkeep rain chances on the lower end through Thursday with\ngradually increasing southerly moisture both at the surface and\naloft on Friday. As mentioned yesterday, a cold front moving\nsoutheast toward the Ohio Valley is advertised to now lose upper\nsupport and stall to our northwest by Friday afternoon. Increased\ndiurnally-driven rain chances would therefore be in the cards for\nFriday through the weekend with highs remaining above normal in\nthe low to mid 90s.\n\n56/GDG",
